  "strategy": "In this strategy, we optimize the minimum overlap problem by carefully balancing global and local search strategies across multiple resolutions to escape local minima and find high-quality solutions. The core idea is to use a sequence of increasing resolutions, each time allowing more refined optimization. The algorithm cycles between global random walk exploration and fine-tuning using local search (L-BFGS-B optimizer), ensuring we always work with feasible candidates satisfying 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and \u222bh(x)dx=1.\n\n**Rationale**  \nThe primary limitation of previous approaches was rigid search processes with fixed resolution sizes. Our method dynamically adjusts the function representation from low to high resolution while maintaining mathematical validity. This reduces the risk of overfitting to any particular representation and allows us to systematically refine solutions across scales. The dual-search (global + local) structure accelerates convergence towards configurations that minimze the maximum correlation.\n\n**Mathematical specification**  \nWe represent h(x) as a piecewise constant function on J equidist  grid points in [0,2]. The objective is:\n\nC\u2085 = max under k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nSubject to:\n  - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n  - \u222b\u2080\u00b2 hdx = 1\n\nWhere the integral is approximated by:\n  \u222b = (2/N) \u2211 h[i] for N-point grid\n  Correlation = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n  C\u2085 = (max correlation at lag) \u00d7 (2/N)\n\n**Implementation procedure**  \n1. **Problem initialization**  \n   - Default resolution: [64, 128, 256, 512, 1024]\n   - Global budget splitting: ~20% for N=64, ~80% for N=1024\n   - Fallback path: retain all parent candidates at each resolution level\n\n2. **Candidate generation**  \n   For each resolution level (N):\n   - Initialize N-dimensional vector with elements of either:\n     a) Parent solution interpolated to N points\n     b) Random initialization: np.random.uniform(0, 1, N)\n   - Adjust to \u222b=1 via project_h() function\n\n3. **Local search**  \n   For each candidate at resolution N:\n   - Use scipy.optimize.minimize(method='L-BFGS-B')\n   - Objective function: smooth_max(correlation_values, beta)\n   - Constraints: project_h() via custom penalty\n   - Step management: shrink in steps (1/N^2) then adaptive gradient clipping\n\n4. **Global search**  \n   Post-local search, allow \"jumps\" in Lag dimensions by:\n   - Deleting and re-growing extreme values (h[i] outside [0,1])\n   - Periodically perturbing solutions via random walks:\n     perturb = np.random.normal(0, 1e-3, N)\n     candidate_trial = current_candidate + perturb\n\n5. **Final polishing**  \n   For final solution at highest resolution (N=1024):\n   - Gradient refinement using SLSQP with \u03b2 targeting \u22651000\n   - Post-processing: verify all values between 0 and 1\n\n**Search choices and useful variations**  \n1. Initial resolution choice:  \n   Default: [64,128,256,512,1024]  \n   Explanation: Computes as 2*N -slowdowns manageable\n\n2. Local optimizer settings:  \n   Method: L-BFGS-B\n   Max iterations: 500\n   Tolerance: 1e-5\n   Bounds: [0,1] for each parameter\n\n3. Smooth_max handling:  \n   calls = np.max(correlation_value) * (2/N)\n   penalty_weight = 5.0 * (N/(N+50))\n   uses exponential transform for monotonicity\n\n4. Abandon early criterion:\n   - 500 iterations with <2% improvement\n   - 20h of elapsed time\n   - 15 local optimization failures\n\n**Computation budget and robustness**  \n1. Resolution time estimates:\n   - N=64: ~150ms (correlation)\n   - N=1024: ~0.5s (correlation using FFT)\n   Total for N=1024: ~60s with gradient calls\n\n2. Observe:\n   - Warmup at N=64 completes in 30s\n   - N>300 use custom FFT-accelerated correlation\n   - Use convergence monitoring at each resolution level\n\n**Validation and return contract**  \nFor final candidate, perform these checks:\n1. \u222bh dx = 1 with tolerance 1e-8\n2. All 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n3. C\u2085 = max( correlation_values ) * (2/N)\n4. Round-trip verification: rescore final candidate\n\nFall back mechanism:  \n- Preserve best candidate from all resolutions\n- Verify all grid points 0\u2264h\u22641 before returning\n- Maintain N-point vector exactly",
